WEBVTT

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:05.000
<v Ira Glass>So Neil Chesanow, do you have a copy of your book, Please Read This for Me, there with you?
<v Ira Glass>那么，尼尔·切萨诺，你手边有你的书《请代我读此书》吗？

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.980
<v Neil Chesanow>Yes, I do, right in my greasy little claw.
<v Neil Chesanow>有的，就在我这双油腻腻的小爪子里。

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:14.240
<v Ira Glass>Perfect. Can I ask you to open up just to the opening page of the preface to read from the preface?
<v Ira Glass>太好了。能请你翻到序言的第一页，给大家读一下序言吗？

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:37.370
<v Neil Chesanow>"When you have something very important but really tough to tell the man in your life, wouldn't it be great if you could just reach for a book that starts the conversation for you? Imagine being able to turn to the appropriate page, give an open book to the man you love, and ask, 'Please read this for me.' 'What is it?' 'Just read it, OK? Page 73. It's only a few lines.'
<v Neil Chesanow>“当你有什么非常重要但又难以启齿的事情要告诉生命中的那个男人时，要是能随手拿起一本书，替你开启这段对话，那该多好？试想一下，当你翻到适当的那一页，把翻开的书递给你爱的男人，对他说：‘请代我读读这个。’‘是什么？’‘读就对了，好吗？第73页，就几行字。’

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:44.240
<v Neil Chesanow>Once the man you love has the book in his hands, a glance will do the job. Each page is an emotional telegram."
<v Neil Chesanow>一旦你爱的男人拿到这本书，只需一眼，任务就完成了。每一页都是一封情感电报。”

00:00:44.240 --> 00:01:12.380
<v Ira Glass>Neil Chesanow's book is now long out of print. But back in the 1980s, when it was published, it represented, I think, a kind of utopian endgame for what self-help books could accomplish. Instead of explaining in a general way how you should handle this situation or that situation in your life, the book, Please Read This to Me, cut to the chase, went to the next logical step. It actually gave you a script. You're in this situation? Say this. Here are the actual words you can use to kick off the conversation you need to have.
<v Ira Glass>尼尔·切萨诺的这本书早已绝版。但回想上世纪80年代它刚出版时，我认为它代表了自助类书籍所能实现的某种乌托邦式的终极目标。这本书没有泛泛地解释你该如何处理生活中这样或那样的情况，而是直击要害，迈出了合乎逻辑的下一步。它实际上是给了你一份剧本。你处于这种状况？那就这么说。这里有你可以直接用来开启对话的现成词句。

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:22.630
<v Neil Chesanow>I tried to come up with a book that would not just tell you how to communicate, but actually spark conversation.
<v Neil Chesanow>我试图创作一本书，不仅是告诉你如何沟通，更能真正地引发对话。

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:52.530
<v Ira Glass>Let me just read some of these titles to give people a sense of what some of these are like. Page 111, "It's time to admit you don't have a drinking problem. You're an alcoholic." Page 82, "You act like Cary Grant in public and Archie Bunker at home." Page 134, "I think about marriage all the time." Page 148, "Maybe I'm not ready to have a baby." Page 136, "You'd probably prefer if I were an orphan." Let me ask you to read the one on page 135.
<v Ira Glass>让我读一下其中的一些标题，让大家感受一下它们是什么样的。第111页，“是时候承认你没有酗酒问题了。你是个酒鬼。”第82页，“你在公共场合表现得像加里·格兰特，在家却像阿奇·邦克。”第134页，“我一直在考虑结婚的事。”第148页，“也许我还没准备好要孩子。”第136页，“你大概宁愿我是一个孤儿。”让我请你读一下第135页的那篇。

00:01:52.530 --> 00:02:17.060
<v Neil Chesanow>OK. This one is entitled, "It could be that I'm falling out of love." A difficult subject. "Once I thought I loved you. Now I'm not so sure. Yes, we've changed over time, but that's only part of it. I feel confused. I don't know what I feel.
<v Neil Chesanow>好的。这一篇的标题是，“也许我正对你失去爱意。”一个沉重的话题。“曾经，我以为我爱你。现在，我不再那么确定了。是的，随着时间的推移我们都变了，但这只是其中一部分原因。我感到困惑。我不知道自己的感受。

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:42.320
<v Neil Chesanow>One minute I say to myself, stick it out. Make it work. Don't be a quitter. And I feel guilty. It's not in my power to change you. That much I've learned. So I'm giving some serious thought to the only alternative left that I can think of-- a trial separation. If you have another suggestion, I'd like to hear it."
<v Neil Chesanow>一分钟前我对自己说，坚持下去。让这段感情继续。别做个逃兵。然后我感到内疚。改变你并不在我的能力范围内。这一点我学到了。所以我正在认真考虑我能想到的唯一选择——分居试验。如果你有其他建议，我很乐意听听。”

00:02:42.320 --> 00:03:13.580
<v Ira Glass>Over and over in this book, Chesanow and his co-author, Gareth Esersky, take some of the most painful situations that people can have with each other and give surprisingly graceful one-minute speeches that a person could say or write or show to somebody else. There are a dozen about problems that people might have together in bed. 10 are about dealing with each other as friends. 10 are about the fights that couples have over money. One of the entries even ends with the sentence, "Let's become husband and wife." All of these are written for women because, Chesanow says, men don't buy these books.
<v Ira Glass>切萨诺和他的合著者加雷斯·埃瑟斯基在书中一遍又一遍地选取人们之间最痛苦的处境，并给出令人惊讶的优雅的一分钟演讲，让人们可以对别人说出、写下或展示出来。其中有十几篇关于人们在床上可能遇到的问题。有10篇是关于如何作为朋友相处。有10篇是关于夫妻间因金钱产生的争吵。其中一篇甚至以“让我们成为夫妻吧”这句话结尾。所有这些都是为女性写的，因为切萨诺说，男人不买这些书。

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:22.460
<v Neil Chesanow>We specifically interviewed women to come up with the most difficult kinds of subjects to discuss.
<v Neil Chesanow>我们专门采访了女性，总结出最难讨论的各种话题。

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:24.080
<v Ira Glass>Let me ask you to read another one.
<v Ira Glass>让我请你再读一篇。

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:25.560
<v Neil Chesanow>I'd be happy to.
<v Neil Chesanow>我很乐意。

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:29.340
<v Ira Glass>Let me ask you to read on page 105.
<v Ira Glass>让我请你读一下第105页。

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:47.510
<v Neil Chesanow>This one is titled, "Let's Get Religion." "There's something missing in our lives. We're so focused on relating to each other that we've overlooked an important aspect of relating to our world. I'm talking about religion, spirituality, God. A belief in God and a spiritual life can add--"
<v Neil Chesanow>这一篇的标题是，“让我们信教吧。”“我们的生活中缺少了什么。我们太专注于彼此之间的关系，却忽略了与世界建立联系的一个重要方面。我说的是宗教、灵性、上帝。对上帝的信仰和精神生活可以增加——”

00:03:47.510 --> 00:04:14.690
<v Ira Glass>When you read one of these after another, the book as a whole seems to have this almost touching faith in the idea that getting the words right might actually solve something or help something. But of course, if you want religion in your life and your partner doesn't, or if you think you've fallen out of love, or if you don't want a baby and your spouse does, I've got to say, your main problem is not what words to use. Your main problem is the situation itself. Neil Chesanow says that plenty of times he heard from women who used the words in the book and it didn't fix the problem.
<v Ira Glass>当你一篇接一篇地读这些内容时，整本书似乎有一种近乎感人的信念，认为只要措辞正确，或许真的能解决或改善某些问题。但当然，如果你想要宗教信仰而你的伴侣不想要，或者如果你认为自己已经不再相爱，或者如果你不想要孩子而你的配偶想要，我必须说，你的主要问题不在于使用什么词汇。你的主要问题是处境本身。尼尔·切萨诺说，他曾多次听到那些使用了书中词句的女性反馈，结果问题并没有得到解决。

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:48.470
<v Neil Chesanow>So there may not be magic words. There aren't always magic words, but sometimes that in itself becomes something vital for a woman to understand. Now she can tell herself, well, I really have given it my best shot. This message in Please Read This for Me really sums up my feelings. He's still not willing to respond. I think we're reaching one of those landmark moments where we have to decide whether we want to go on or not.
<v Neil Chesanow>所以，可能根本就没有什么魔法词汇。魔法词汇并不总是存在，但有时这本身就成了女性需要理解的重要一点。现在她可以告诉自己，好吧，我已经尽我所能了。《请代我读此书》中的这段话确实总结了我的感受。他仍然不愿意回应。我想我们正到达一个标志性的时刻，必须决定我们是否还要继续走下去。

00:04:48.470 --> 00:05:12.450
<v Ira Glass>Which brings us to today's radio program. Today, during this week in which talking is failing on an international level, from Washington and Moscow to Paris and Baghdad, we bring you stories that ask the question, what is talking good for, anyway? When does it work? When doesn't it work? From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Our program today in four acts.
<v Ira Glass>这就引出了今天的广播节目。在谈话在国际层面上遭遇挫折的这一周里，从华盛顿到莫斯科，再到巴黎和巴格达，我们为你带来了一些故事，探讨一个问题：谈话到底有什么用？它什么时候有效？什么时候无效？这里是WBEZ芝加哥的《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。我们今天的节目分为四个篇章。

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:31.410
<v Ira Glass>Act One, How to Write a Note. In that act, a guy who thinks that words and reason might help his friend from trying to hurt himself. Act Two, The Battle of Words Versus Fear. In that act, one man decides to conquer his fears by listing them, all of them, one after another, all 183 of them.
<v Ira Glass>第一章，如何写便条。在那一章里，一个人认为言语和理性能帮助他的朋友打消伤害自己的念头。第二章，言语与恐惧之战。在那一章里，一个人决定通过列出自己的恐惧来战胜它们，所有这些恐惧，一个接一个，总共183个。

00:05:31.410 --> 00:06:09.350
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, When a City Opens Its Big Mouth. The story of just how easy-- how astonishingly easy-- it is to get people of all ages and races and economic levels to open up and chat. You just need three little words. Act Four, Wedding Bells and Telephone Bells. In that act, Jonathan Goldstein and Liz Gilbert bring us stories of words failing-- in one case, in the worst wedding toast that any of us has ever heard of occurring at a real wedding. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>第三章，当城市张开大嘴。这个故事讲述了让所有年龄、种族和经济阶层的人敞开心扉聊天是多么容易——多么令人震惊的容易。你只需要三个小词。第四章，婚礼的钟声与电话的铃声。在那一章里，乔纳森·戈尔茨坦和利兹·吉尔伯特为我们带来了关于言语失效的故事——其中一个案例是，我们所听说过的在真实婚礼上发生的最糟糕的婚礼祝酒词。请继续收听。

00:06:09.350 --> 00:06:12.320
<v Ira Glass>Act One, How to Write a Note.
<v Ira Glass>第一章，如何写便条。

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:25.220
<v Ira Glass>This first story on our show today is unusual. It wasn't originally made to be broadcast on any radio show. It was a tape made by somebody who had never put anything together for radio. He made it to give to a friend. Here's what happened.
<v Ira Glass>今天节目中的第一个故事非常不寻常。它原本不是为了在任何广播节目中播出而制作的。这是一盘录音带，由一个从未为广播做过任何内容的人制作。他制作它是为了送给一个朋友。事情是这样的。

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:48.830
<v Ira Glass>Back in July of 1999, Jake Warga heard that somebody who he was close to in college, his friend Brian, tried to kill himself. Jake went out to visit Brian and he took with him this little MiniDisc recorder that he had just bought for himself that had this little clip-on microphone. During his visit with Brian, they recorded this long conversation they had. And then when Jake got home, he decided to edit this down and give it to Brian as a present.
<v Ira Glass>1999年7月，杰克·瓦尔加听说他在大学时的一位挚友布莱恩试图自杀。杰克去探望布莱恩，随身带着他刚给自己买的一个小型迷你光盘录音机，上面还带着一个小夹式麦克风。在探望布莱恩期间，他们录下了那场长谈。杰克回家后，决定将其剪辑成片，作为礼物送给布莱恩。

00:06:48.830 --> 00:07:21.650
<v Ira Glass>He had no idea how to actually edit sound or do anything like that, so at some point, he jumped onto the internet and found a website-- a website, actually, that we've mentioned on our show in the past, called transom.org that teaches beginners how to edit and mix audio with links to free editing software. And Jake put together this story you're about to hear. And he sent it to Brian. His hope was that if Brian heard this tape, heard himself talking, heard his own words, it might convince him that he shouldn't try to kill himself again. That didn't work. Here's the tape that Jake put together.
<v Ira Glass>他根本不知道该如何剪辑声音，也不知道怎么做这些事，所以他在某个时候上网找到了一个网站——事实上，这个网站我们以前在节目中提到过，叫transom.org，它教初学者如何剪辑和混合音频，并附有免费剪辑软件的链接。杰克整理出了你即将听到的这个故事。他把它发给了布莱恩。他的希望是，如果布莱恩听到这盘录音带，听到自己的声音，听到自己的话语，或许能让他相信不应该再试图自杀。但这没起作用。以下是杰克整理出来的录音带。

00:07:21.650 --> 00:07:58.130
<v Jake Warga>Last year, my friend Brian tried to commit suicide. He had checked himself into a new hotel that runs alongside the interstate, which happens to pass through the small college town in which he lives. And without ceremony or note-writing, he took a combination of drugs he thought sufficient enough to quietly end his life.
<v Jake Warga>去年，我的朋友布莱恩试图自杀。他在州际公路旁的一家新旅馆开了房，这条公路恰好经过他居住的大学城。他没有举行任何仪式，也没有留下便条，而是服用了一种他认为足以让他静静结束生命的药物组合。

00:07:58.130 --> 00:08:26.150
<v Jake Warga>As people often do, Brian and I started getting lazy about communicating after I left college and moved out of that same college town. Emails became rare, and phone calls rarer. After the longest period yet of not hearing from Brian, I got a call from an old mutual friend of ours asking if I had seen or heard from him. I said I had not.
<v Jake Warga>就像人们常做的那样，我离开大学并搬出那个大学城后，布莱恩和我开始疏于沟通。电子邮件变得稀少，电话更少。在经历了我与布莱恩失联最长的一段时间后，我接到了我们一位老朋友的电话，问我是否见过或听过他的消息。我说没有。

00:08:26.150 --> 00:08:54.180
<v Jake Warga>We made calls and eventually found him safe in the hospital. A few weeks later, I arranged a visit to see how my old school-- and Brian-- had changed in the years I had been away. And when I came to visit, we sat for three hours on a park bench late one summer night. He was still in the process of piecing together exactly what had happened. This is Brian.
<v Jake Warga>我们拨打了电话，最终发现他在医院里很安全。几周后，我安排了一次访问，想看看我离开的这些年里，我的母校——以及布莱恩——发生了什么变化。当我去看望他时，在一个夏天的深夜，我们在公园的长椅上坐了三个小时。他当时仍在整理到底发生了什么。这是布莱恩。

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:56.690
<v Jake Warga>Let's talk about you.
<v Jake Warga>我们谈谈你吧。

00:08:56.690 --> 00:09:02.240
<v Brian>There's not much to talk about.
<v Brian>没什么好谈的。

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:05.020
<v Jake Warga>You were going to go to the hospital today, right?
<v Jake Warga>你今天要去医院，对吧？

00:09:05.020 --> 00:09:44.990
<v Brian>Oh, yeah. I did. Yeah. The woman in the records department made a photocopy of my medical records from when I got admitted, and there wasn't anything too surprising or anything in there. But it was still interesting to read. But they said I was discovered at approximately 6:00 PM. I guess this was the next day.
<v Brian>哦，是的。我去了。病案科的女士复印了我入院时的病历，里面没有什么太令人惊讶的内容。但读起来还是很有趣。他们说我大约在下午6点被发现。我想这是第二天了。

00:09:44.990 --> 00:09:46.320
<v Jake Warga>6:00 PM?
<v Jake Warga>下午6点？

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:47.880
<v Brian>Yeah. I don't know why--
<v Brian>是的。我不知道为什么——

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:49.570
<v Jake Warga>They waited so long?
<v Jake Warga>他们等了那么久？

00:09:49.570 --> 00:10:17.230
<v Brian>Yeah. Because I should have checked out around noon, I guess. But they said I was unconscious and unresponsive. I was reading the paramedics report. I was pale, cold, and clammy, and I was breathing only six respirations per minute, which is very slow.
<v Brian>是的。因为我想我本应该在中午左右退房的。但他们说我当时处于昏迷且无反应状态。我在读急救人员的报告。我脸色苍白、身体冰冷湿冷，呼吸只有每分钟六次，非常缓慢。

00:10:17.230 --> 00:10:48.000
<v Brian>And they said they found pill bottles and a bottle of alcohol in the room. And they cleared my airway and started administering oxygen. Then they gave me a 2 milliliters IV of something called Narcan, which is an antidote for opiates like morphine.
<v Brian>他们说在房间里发现了药瓶和一瓶酒。他们清理了我的呼吸道并开始供氧。然后他们给我静脉注射了2毫升名为纳洛酮的药物，这是吗啡等阿片类药物的解毒剂。

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:49.800
<v Jake Warga>Do you think it would have worked?
<v Jake Warga>你觉得这奏效了吗？

00:10:49.800 --> 00:11:29.170
<v Brian>I think it would have. And I mean, maybe I didn't take enough because morphine is very serious. It's very hard core. It's very easy to OD on it. (JOKINGLY) It's hard core, man. And I guess it was really late that night or even early the next morning when I guess they were about to transfer me, and I woke up. And the attendant who wrote this report said that I said I was disappointed to be alive and that I had passed out before I could take the morphine.
<v Brian>我觉得奏效了。我的意思是，也许我服用的量不够，因为吗啡非常严重。它是硬核的。服用过量非常容易。（开玩笑地说）这很硬核，伙计。我想那是那天深夜甚至是第二天清晨，他们准备转院时我醒了。写这份报告的护理员说我当时表示很失望自己还活着，而且我在服用吗啡之前就已经昏过去了。

00:11:29.170 --> 00:12:00.680
<v Brian>And that part I do remember really vaguely. I remember waking up and feeling the nasal cannula in my nose giving me oxygen and seeing that IV bottle hovering over me. And the guy asked me, kind of sarcastically it sounded, so are you glad to be alive? And I'm pretty sure I remember saying no.
<v Brian>那部分我记得非常模糊。我记得醒来时感觉到鼻导管在给我供氧，看到那瓶静脉注射液悬在上方。那个人问我，听起来有点讽刺，问我是不是很高兴还活着？我很确定我记得我说不。

00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:30.000
<v Jake Warga>Brian doesn't have that many friends. He's good-looking and funny, yet something inside prevents him from being confident in social skills. For example, it took a long time in our friendship before he told me, and I felt confident in asking, about his biological mother. He told me she died when he was young, that she had committed suicide. His father remarried soon after and raised Brian and his brother, who is now a doctor.
<v Jake Warga>布莱恩没几个朋友。他长得帅又风趣，但内心深处有什么东西阻碍了他建立社交自信。例如，在我们的友谊持续很长一段时间后，他才告诉我关于他亲生母亲的事，而我也才感到有把握去问。他告诉我，她在他小时候就去世了，她是自杀身亡的。他父亲不久后改组了家庭，抚养大了布莱恩和他的哥哥，现在哥哥是一位医生。

00:12:30.000 --> 00:13:11.430
<v Brian>Although, there seemed to be some incongruities with the reports. I guess those paramedics and hospital staff can really hastily fill stuff out. They can really take a lot of license. My own mom's coroner's report was really-- it had some gross inconsistencies in it, or errors. My brother says coroners often just make stuff up if they can't find certain causes, like cause of death, I guess.
<v Brian>尽管报告中似乎存在一些矛盾之处。我想那些急救人员和医务人员填表时确实很匆忙。他们确实能动用很大的自由裁量权。我母亲的验尸报告真的很——它有一些严重的出入或错误。我哥哥说，如果验尸官无法确定某些死因，他们往往只是瞎编。

00:13:11.430 --> 00:13:15.290
<v Jake Warga>Were you hoping to find similarities between your mom's report and your report?
<v Jake Warga>你当时是希望能从你母亲的报告和你的报告中找到相似之处吗？

00:13:15.290 --> 00:13:42.170
<v Brian>Oh, no. I hadn't really thought of that. I brought her up just because they made some errors on hers, just like they had with mine. But with me, I don't think it was anything really major.
<v Brian>哦，不。我没往那方面想。我提到她只是因为她的报告上有错误，就像他们在我身上犯错一样。但对我来说，我不认为有什么真正重大的问题。

00:13:42.170 --> 00:14:04.190
<v Jake Warga>About a week prior to this interview, Brian was arrested in San Francisco for possession of narcotics. Brian does not use drugs or alcohol. He had gone to the same bad part of town to buy the same drugs with the intent of trying again. I asked him about this trip to San Francisco.
<v Jake Warga>在这次采访前大约一周，布莱恩在旧金山因持有毒品被捕。布莱恩不吸毒也不喝酒。他去过那个糟糕的城市街区，为了购买同样的药物，意图再试一次。我问了他这次旧金山之行。

00:14:04.190 --> 00:14:07.250
<v Jake Warga>What were you doing there?
<v Jake Warga>你去那里干什么？

00:14:07.250 --> 00:14:10.740
<v Brian>Same thing.
<v Brian>一样的事情。

00:14:10.740 --> 00:14:12.360
<v Jake Warga>Same cocktail?
<v Jake Warga>同样的鸡尾酒疗法？

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:15.430
<v Brian>Mhm. Pretty much.
<v Brian>嗯。差不多。

00:14:15.430 --> 00:14:18.330
<v Jake Warga>But it didn't work before.
<v Jake Warga>但之前没奏效。

00:14:18.330 --> 00:14:47.500
<v Brian>I'm too much of a wimp to try other things, like other things that might be more violent but less immediate. I don't like violence in practice.
<v Brian>我太胆小了，不敢尝试其他东西，比如那些可能更暴力但不那么直接的方法。我不喜欢在实践中搞暴力。

00:14:47.500 --> 00:15:10.040
<v Jake Warga>I've read that suicide is a selfish act, yet I have never really thought of Brian as selfish. But I can understand why the relatives of suicide victims might go through that angry phase, that phase when they place the blame on the person who killed themselves to help with any feelings of guilt they might have. I asked Brian if he thought what he did was selfish.
<v Jake Warga>我读过自杀是一种自私的行为，但我从来没觉得布莱恩自私。但我能理解为什么自杀受害者的亲属会经历那个愤怒的阶段，那个把责任归咎于自杀者以减轻内心愧疚感的阶段。我问布莱恩，他是否认为自己的行为是自私的。

00:15:10.040 --> 00:15:36.530
<v Brian>I say it's selfish in the same sense that going to a therapist is selfish. You have a problem and you're doing something about it. You're doing something about it in the way you feel like coping with it.
<v Brian>我说它是自私的，就像去看心理医生是自私的一样。你遇到了问题，并且你在采取行动。你以你觉得能够应对它的方式在采取行动。

00:15:36.530 --> 00:16:11.340
<v Brian>I don't think, in the end, people should live for other people. They should really live for themselves, just like you shouldn't go to school for a decade to become a doctor or a lawyer or whatever just because your parents want you to be that because you want to do what your parents want you to do. You should live your life as you want, as you see fit.
<v Brian>归根结底，我认为人们不应该为别人而活。他们应该真正为自己而活，就像你不应该为了迎合父母的意愿去读十年的医学院或法学院，只因为你想做父母想让你做的事。你应该按你想要的方式、按你认为合适的方式过你的生活。

00:16:11.340 --> 00:16:45.210
<v Brian>And it doesn't necessarily mean you haven't considered other people's reactions. It just means that maybe you have and you've decided that, unfortunately, you still want to go forward with it. I don't want to spend the rest of my life alive and miserable just because someone else doesn't want me to die. I don't see much sense to that.
<v Brian>而且这并不一定意味着你没有考虑过其他人的反应。这只是意味着也许你考虑过了，但不幸的是，你仍然决定继续。我不想因为别人不希望我死，就余生都活在痛苦中。我觉得这没什么意义。

00:16:45.210 --> 00:16:58.580
<v Jake Warga>In an effort to catch up on letter-writing, Brian would occasionally write long emails filled with wit, humor, and sometimes desperation. He's an excellent writer. I asked him why he didn't write a suicide note.
<v Jake Warga>为了弥补书信往来，布莱恩偶尔会写很长的电子邮件，充满机智、幽默，有时也充满绝望。他是个出色的作家。我问他为什么不写遗书。

00:16:58.580 --> 00:17:39.380
<v Brian>I'll admit that was rather selfish. I know that people were wondering what the heck is up. I could have sat down for probably what would have been several hours to type something up. At that point in time, I was just very fed up and impatient and just I wanted it all to end. I just didn't want to screw around with anything else. I could've also cleaned my room first, too.
<v Brian>我承认那确实相当自私。我知道人们想知道到底发生了什么。我本可以坐下来，花上几个小时打点什么出来。但当时，我只是受够了，非常不耐烦，我只想这一切都结束。我不想再折腾任何事了。我也本可以先打扫一下房间。

00:17:39.380 --> 00:17:43.950
<v Jake Warga>As we were talking, an ambulance passed by in no particular hurry.
<v Jake Warga>当我们交谈时，一辆救护车不紧不慢地驶过。

00:17:43.950 --> 00:17:50.720
<v Brian>There's what I probably rode in. It cost over $800 to be transported from--
<v Brian>我大概就是坐这辆车走的。从那儿到这儿的运送费用超过800美元——

00:17:50.720 --> 00:17:51.310
<v Jake Warga>A few blocks?
<v Jake Warga>几个街区？

00:17:51.310 --> 00:17:59.690
<v Brian>Yeah. According to their time table, it took them eight minutes to get from there to there.
<v Brian>是的。按照他们的时间表，他们花八分钟从那儿到这儿。

00:17:59.690 --> 00:18:01.450
<v Jake Warga>I guess that's pretty far.
<v Jake Warga>我想那挺远的。

00:18:01.450 --> 00:18:15.150
<v Brian>Yeah, that's an appreciable distance, I guess. It's worth eight minutes. 2 milligrams, 2 tiny milligrams of Narcan opiate antidote is $6.
<v Brian>是的，那是一段相当长的距离。八分钟的时间确实值这个钱。2毫克，2小毫克的纳洛酮阿片类解毒剂只要6美元。

00:18:15.150 --> 00:18:33.560
<v Jake Warga>As I write this, I wonder if I'm making Brian's note for him. Am I documenting this story for him, or for whom he might leave behind? This was not the first time he tried committing suicide, and, in light of his recent arrest, not his last.
<v Jake Warga>当我写下这些时，我在想我是否在替布莱恩写遗书。我是在为他记录这个故事，还是为了他离世后留下的那些人？这已经不是他第一次试图自杀，而且考虑到他最近被捕，这也不是最后一次。

00:18:33.560 --> 00:18:35.940
<v Jake Warga>Do you think you found the only way to cope?
<v Jake Warga>你认为你找到了应对的唯一方法吗？

00:18:35.940 --> 00:19:02.800
<v Brian>The only viable way, so to speak. I don't get the impression most people are that happy, anyway. They just grind their way through life. They'll have kids, and that'll give them an artificial reason to live for a while. And then the kids grow up and forget about them.
<v Brian>唯一可行的方法，可以这么说。反正我觉得大多数人也没那么快乐。他们只是在生活中苦苦挣扎。他们会有孩子，那会给他们一个暂时的、人为的活着的理由。然后孩子们长大了，就把他们忘了。

00:19:02.800 --> 00:19:39.720
<v Brian>I know the mind is a really powerful thing. People can do just about anything they really put their minds to, but it also takes a tremendous amount of self-motivation. As my therapist says, it has to come from within, and it doesn't feel like there's much within.
<v Brian>我知道思想是非常强大的东西。人们只要下定决心，几乎可以做任何事，但这也需要巨大的自我激励。正如我的心理医生所说，它必须来自内心，而我感觉内心并没有多少东西。

00:19:39.720 --> 00:19:44.830
<v Jake Warga>What's your relationship with death?
<v Jake Warga>你和死亡的关系是什么？

00:19:44.830 --> 00:20:50.950
<v Brian>I was brought up in a Protestant family as a Lutheran, and I haven't renounced that faith. I just feel I've stumbled in a big way, and I haven't gotten up or haven't been able to get up. And I'm hoping that I'll just go to heaven after I die. But of course, there is a lingering fear of hell because it's not a-- it's highly stigmatized, suicide. But I don't think I believe in sins that are, what do you call them? Cardinal. Yeah. I think it's just another kind of sin.
<v Brian>我在一个信奉路德教的新教家庭中长大，我没有背弃那种信仰。我只是觉得我摔得很惨，还没能爬起来，或者说无法爬起来。我希望死后能去天堂。但当然，对地狱存在着挥之不去的恐惧，因为——自杀是被高度污名化的。但我认为我不相信有什么所谓的“大罪”。是的。我觉得那只是另一种罪。

00:20:50.950 --> 00:21:15.590
<v Brian>My cousin, Amy, who's an atheist, told me that her dad, who's a Lutheran minister, told her that, if you kill yourself, you go to hell because you're not alive to repent and ask forgiveness for that sin. So therefore, since you have not repented for that sin, you'll go to hell.
<v Brian>我的表妹艾米是个无神论者，她告诉我，她父亲，一位路德教会的牧师，曾告诉她，如果你自杀，你会下地狱，因为你活着的时候没有忏悔并请求宽恕那种罪。所以，因为你没有为那种罪忏悔，你会下地狱。

00:21:15.590 --> 00:21:41.930
<v Brian>And I think that's ridiculous. Just think of all the sins you haven't repented for in your life, even if you tried to. I know you haven't tried to, but-- so I do have a fear of dying, but it doesn't always outweigh my other fears or my other--
<v Brian>我认为这很荒谬。想想你一生中那些没有忏悔过的罪吧，即使你想忏悔。我知道你还没试过，但是——所以我确实有死亡的恐惧，但它并不总是盖过我其他的恐惧或——

00:21:41.930 --> 00:21:43.820
<v Jake Warga>Fear of living?
<v Jake Warga>活着的恐惧？

00:21:43.820 --> 00:21:51.320
<v Brian>Yeah.
<v Brian>是的。

00:21:51.320 --> 00:22:07.700
<v Jake Warga>After sitting at a park bench for so long, and after Brian confessed to having talked more than he ever has, we were more than overdue for a stretch. And after a while of walking around aimlessly, we picked up our old habit of trainspotting and penny-smashing.
<v Jake Warga>在公园长椅上坐了那么久，在布莱恩承认说了比以往任何时候都多的话之后，我们早就该伸展一下筋骨了。在漫无目的地走了一会儿后，我们又恢复了旧习惯：看火车和压硬币。

00:22:07.700 --> 00:22:08.620
<v Jake Warga>[TRAIN WHISTLE BLOWING]
<v Jake Warga>[火车汽笛声]

00:22:08.620 --> 00:22:09.890
<v Brian>Jeez.
<v Brian>天哪。

00:22:09.890 --> 00:22:10.510
<v Jake Warga>Quick.
<v Jake Warga>快。

00:22:10.510 --> 00:22:14.870
<v Brian>I would have loved to--
<v Brian>我本希望——

00:22:14.870 --> 00:22:17.690
<v Jake Warga>You're taking it slow.
<v Jake Warga>你跑得太慢了。

00:22:17.690 --> 00:22:22.040
<v Brian>I probably shouldn't run with this thing. I don't know if I can record while being jostled.
<v Brian>我大概不应该拿着这东西跑。我不知道在颠簸的时候能不能录音。

00:22:22.040 --> 00:22:23.990
<v Jake Warga>It'll catch up.
<v Jake Warga>它会跟上的。

00:22:23.990 --> 00:22:30.970
<v Brian>[LAUGHS]
<v Brian>[笑声]

00:22:30.970 --> 00:22:45.480
<v Brian>We are now running to try to catch the train. We're running past lions. We're now up on the tracks. Here come the lights.
<v Brian>我们现在跑着试图赶上火车。我们跑过狮子像。我们现在跑到了铁轨上。灯光来了。

00:22:45.480 --> 00:22:50.010
<v Jake Warga>At this point, Brian and I are running alongside the train as it's beginning to stop at the nearby station.
<v Jake Warga>此时，布莱恩和我正沿着火车跑，它开始在附近的车站停下。

00:22:50.010 --> 00:22:52.500
<v Jake Warga>[TRAIN RUNNING]
<v Jake Warga>[火车行进声]

00:22:52.500 --> 00:23:21.380
<v Jake Warga>[SCREAMING]
<v Jake Warga>[尖叫声]

00:23:21.380 --> 00:23:30.360
<v Jake Warga>[TRAIN HALTING]
<v Jake Warga>[火车停止声]

00:23:30.360 --> 00:23:41.930
<v Jake Warga>Well, train's here. No pennies.
<v Jake Warga>好了，火车到了。没压到硬币。

00:23:41.930 --> 00:23:47.240
<v Brian>Oh, brother. I have a penny or two.
<v Brian>噢，兄弟。我有一两枚硬币。

00:23:47.240 --> 00:23:48.680
<v Jake Warga>Give it up.
<v Jake Warga>给吧。

00:23:48.680 --> 00:24:18.370
<v Brian>You give it up, homeboy.
<v Brian>你给吧，伙计。

00:24:18.370 --> 00:24:21.850
<v Brian>I don't know. Anything else?
<v Brian>我不知道。还有什么吗？

00:24:21.850 --> 00:24:25.250
<v Jake Warga>Do you ever cry?
<v Jake Warga>你哭过吗？

00:24:25.250 --> 00:24:45.170
<v Brian>No. I hardly remember the last time. It's probably been almost 10 years. I came close, though, in December of '92 when I came home after visiting my grandma over Christmastime. My grandma had shown us a bunch of pictures of my biological mom and told us some stories about her.
<v Brian>没有。我几乎不记得上一次是什么时候了。那大概是快10年前了。不过，1992年12月圣诞节期间我去探望祖母回到家时，我确实快哭了。我祖母向我们展示了一堆我亲生母亲的照片，并讲了一些关于她的故事。

00:24:45.170 --> 00:25:03.240
<v Brian>These were photos I don't think I'd ever seen and stories I don't think I'd ever heard. I came home and I was taking a shower late that night, and while I was in the shower, I wept just a little bit. But it wasn't really full-fledged.
<v Brian>这些照片我想我以前从未见过，这些故事我也从未听过。我回到家，那天深夜洗澡的时候，我在淋浴间哭了一点点。但那并不是真正的彻底大哭。

00:25:03.240 --> 00:25:31.250
<v Brian>I kind of feel like I'm emotionally constipated. I guess that's a major part of my problem. People need to express their anger and whatever else they're feeling. I've actually tried to. I've tried because I knew I wanted the release because I knew it would feel good. But I just couldn't do it. Very frustrating.
<v Brian>我感觉自己好像在情绪上便秘了。我想这是我问题的一个重要部分。人们需要表达他们的愤怒以及他们感受到的任何其他东西。我其实试过。我试过，因为我知道我想要那种释放，因为我知道那会感觉很好。但我就是做不到。非常沮丧。

00:25:31.250 --> 00:25:35.120
<v Jake Warga>We're in the last nine minutes of this cassette.
<v Jake Warga>磁带只剩下最后九分钟了。

00:25:35.120 --> 00:25:37.620
<v Brian>(FAKE WHINY) Oh, there's nine more minutes.
<v Brian>（假装抱怨）哦，还有九分钟。

00:25:37.620 --> 00:25:39.870
<v Jake Warga>(FAKE WHINY) I kind of want it over now.
<v Jake Warga>（假装抱怨）我现在只想让它结束。

00:25:39.870 --> 00:26:33.500
<v Brian>I can't think of anything else to say. I'm not the kind of guy who can just rattle off his famous last words or big words of advice. Don't have kids unless you had a good relationship with your own parents, I guess, because you can seriously screw them up by saying negative things to them or even neglecting them. Apparently, that's a highly debated cause of sudden infant death syndrome-- not giving your baby enough physical attention.
<v Brian>我想不出还有什么可说的。我不是那种可以随口说出遗言或重大建议的人。我觉得，除非你和自己的父母关系很好，否则别生孩子，因为你可能会通过对他们说负面的话，甚至忽视他们来严重伤害他们。显然，这是一个关于婴儿猝死综合征的高度争议性原因——没有给婴儿足够的身体关注。

00:26:33.500 --> 00:27:02.630
<v Brian>One of my psychiatrists said I'm failing to thrive. That's a phrase commonly used for infants who mysteriously die. I'm thinking-- I just kind of amuse myself with the thought that my case is a belated case of sudden infant death syndrome.
<v Brian>我的一个精神科医生说我“发育迟缓”。这是常用于神秘死亡的婴儿的一个词组。我在想——我只是用这个想法来自娱自乐：我的情况是迟来的婴儿猝死综合征。

00:27:02.630 --> 00:27:38.040
<v Brian>I don't think my dad failed as a parent, as he worries that he has. I think he did a great job. It's just, along the way, I contracted a disease and it doesn't have a very optimistic or bright prognosis. And not even the best parent can prevent that.
<v Brian>我不认为我父亲作为父母失败了，尽管他担心他失败了。我觉得他做得很好。只是，在这一过程中，我感染了一种疾病，而且它的预后并不乐观或明朗。甚至最好的父母也无法预防这一点。

00:27:38.040 --> 00:27:56.670
<v Jake Warga>At the end of editing this story, Brian is still alive, living in the same small town. I don't know if he's getting better or not.
<v Jake Warga>剪辑完这个故事时，布莱恩还活着，住在同一个小镇。我不知道他是否好转了。

00:27:56.670 --> 00:28:17.570
<v Ira Glass>Jake Warga. He sent this tape to his friend Brian in 1999 and waited for a reaction. Brian emailed him. He wrote, "How did you learn about the music that you included in the interviews?" He liked some of the pieces, but said they, quote, "Might be a tad overdramatic." And he wanted to know if Jake's computer had a filter that can make him sound less stupid.
<v Ira Glass>杰克·瓦尔加。他于1999年将这盘录音带寄给了他的朋友布莱恩并等待回应。布莱恩给他发了电子邮件。他写道：“你是怎么学到你采访中加入的那些音乐的？”他喜欢其中的一些曲子，但说它们，引用他的话说，“可能有点过度戏剧化了。”他还想知道杰克的电脑是否有过滤器可以让他听起来不那么蠢。

00:28:17.570 --> 00:28:36.530
<v Ira Glass>Brian also asked Jake if he had any plans to publish this story. He told Jake that he thought Jake would have an easier time publishing it if he were dead. Nearly two years later, in the spring of 2001, Brian did try to kill himself again, and this time he didn't survive. Jake added this epilogue to the story.
<v Ira Glass>布莱恩还问杰克是否有打算发表这个故事。他告诉杰克，如果他死了，杰克发表这个故事会容易得多。将近两年后的2001年春天，布莱恩确实再次试图自杀，这一次他没能活下来。杰克在这个故事的结尾添加了这段尾声。

00:28:36.530 --> 00:29:07.360
<v Jake Warga>On May 9 of this year, Brian's body was found in his room. He had injected himself with a lethal dose of morphine. He was 31 years old. I had sent this tape to Brian sometime after our interview in hopes that, by hearing himself, like looking long and hard into a mirror, he would realize what he was saying, that he would snap out of it. Though he appreciated my efforts, I did not change him. And I came closer to realizing I never could.
<v Jake Warga>今年的5月9日，布莱恩的尸体在他的房间里被发现。他给自己注射了致死剂量的吗啡。他当时31岁。我在我们采访后不久把这盘录音带寄给了布莱恩，希望通过听到自己的声音，就像对着镜子长久且仔细地审视一样，他会意识到自己在说什么，从而从那种状态中走出来。虽然他感谢我的努力，但我没有改变他。我愈发意识到，我永远也做不到。

00:29:07.360 --> 00:29:26.110
<v Jake Warga>Brian left packets for a few people, myself included. In them were copies of letters he wrote years before, explaining some of what he felt at the time, and tapes of the interview we did that night. He actually had to make cassette copies for his brother and cousin from the MiniDiscs that I sent him.
<v Jake Warga>布莱恩留下了几个包裹，其中也包括给我的。里面有他几年前写的信的复印件，解释了他当时的一些感受，还有我们那晚采访的录音。他实际上是从我寄给他的迷你光盘中为他的哥哥和表妹制作了磁带备份。

00:29:26.110 --> 00:29:51.710
<v Jake Warga>I've had to ask myself now that Brian's dead, why do I want to share this tape? One reason is I want to take something from death, to rob it, for a little while, of the mute it imposes. I also hope that it might help someone who feels like Brian. Or, for friends and family left confused, some sense of closure they may not have had if death were allowed its silence.
<v Jake Warga>布莱恩死后，我不得不问自己，我为什么要分享这盘录音带？一个原因是我想从死亡中夺走一些东西，在那一刻，打破它强加的沉默。我也希望它能帮助那些感觉像布莱恩一样的人。或者，对于那些陷入困惑的朋友和家人，我希望这能带给他们一些如果让死亡保持沉默所无法得到的解脱感。

00:29:51.710 --> 00:30:04.860
<v Jake Warga>So Brian, I'm relieved your pains are over. And now it's time for ours to begin. You will be missed.
<v Jake Warga>所以布莱恩，我为你不再痛苦感到欣慰。现在，轮到我们开始痛苦了。我们会想念你的。

00:30:04.860 --> 00:30:26.320
<v Ira Glass>Jake Warga. He teaches storytelling at Stanford University. Thanks to Jay Allison and transom.org and KUOW in Seattle, where Jake's story has appeared. If you or somebody you know might need help, the National Suicide Prevention Lifeline is available 24/7 at 1-800-273-TALK. That's 1-800-273-8255.
