00:44:16.870 --> 00:44:20.070
<v Woman 1>Who are you and what for?
<v Woman 1>你们是谁，为了什么？

00:44:20.070 --> 00:44:23.610
<v Bill>I'm Bill, and that's Liz, and it's just the two of us.
<v Bill>我是比尔，她是利兹，就我们两个人。

00:44:23.610 --> 00:44:41.620
<v Ira Glass>Here they are, people who decided that it might be nice if strangers would just interact a little more. And they were going to take their matters into their own hands to see that it happen, even though it pays no money, even though it means camping on people's couches, even though they end up acting like cheerful customer service representatives to a largely indifferent world, one exhausting hour after another.
<v Ira Glass>在这里，他们决定如果陌生人能多交流一点会很好。他们决定亲自动手来看看能不能实现，即使这没有任何报酬，即使这意味着要睡在别人的沙发上，即使他们最终在一个很大程度上冷漠的世界里表现得像个开朗的客服代表，一个小时接一个小时地精疲力竭。

00:44:41.620 --> 00:44:45.520
<v Bill>We just put up this sign and anything people want to talk about, we'll go with it.
<v Bill>我们只是立起这个牌子，人们想谈什么，我们就跟他们谈什么。

00:44:45.520 --> 00:44:46.930
<v Man 2>Sign?
<v Man 2>牌子？

00:44:46.930 --> 00:45:01.410
<v Ira Glass>Liz is 25. Bill is 23. But they each use the kind of vernacular that you'd expect from an 80-year-old woman. They call men fellas. And instead of using the word ass or bum to describe the part of the body that you sit on, they favor the word patootie.
<v Ira Glass>利兹25岁，比尔23岁。但他们每个人都使用那种你可能从80岁老太太口中听到的方言。他们称男人为“小伙子”。而且，比起用那个词来形容你坐着的身体部位，他们更偏爱用“屁股”这个词。

00:45:01.410 --> 00:45:17.790
<v Ira Glass>They smile easily. They look young and vulnerable and almost overwhelmingly earnest. That kind of thing either works for you, or it doesn't. And for them, apparently it does. People talk to them. The sign does its job. Two plainclothes cops approach, and the one in the wraparound sunglasses talks first.
<v Ira Glass>他们容易微笑。他们看起来年轻、脆弱，几乎极其真诚。这种事情要么对你有用，要么没用。而对他们来说，显然有用。人们和他们说话。牌子起了作用。两个便衣警察走近，戴着环绕式墨镜的那个先开了口。

00:45:17.790 --> 00:45:24.220
<v Cop 1>This girl I've been going out with for two years just got engaged to some dude after dating him for four weeks.
<v Cop 1>我谈了两年的女朋友在和另一个人约会四个星期后就订婚了。

00:45:24.220 --> 00:45:28.730
<v Liz>Did you think she was just spending your time? Did you think she was stringing you along? Do you think she didn't love you?
<v Liz>你觉得她是在浪费你的时间吗？你觉得她一直在吊着你吗？你觉得她不爱你吗？

00:45:28.730 --> 00:45:33.990
<v Cop 1>Yeah, sure, for like the last six months. Yeah. Nice, right?
<v Cop 1>是的，当然，大概有六个月了。很好，对吧？

00:45:33.990 --> 00:45:35.380
<v Liz>Did you know?
<v Liz>你知道吗？

00:45:35.380 --> 00:45:38.880
<v Cop 1>No. I had no idea. Nope.
<v Cop 1>不。我一点都不知道。不。

00:45:38.880 --> 00:45:52.000
<v Ira Glass>In the course of this one day in New York City, Liz and Bill chat with a teenager from LaGuardia High School who was all excited about the fake country she and her friends had made up. There was a drunk guy carrying bags full of brand new computer equipment. He talked about how much money he's making.
<v Ira Glass>在纽约市的这一天里，利兹和比尔与拉瓜迪亚高中的一个青少年聊了天，她对她和朋友们编造的假想国非常兴奋。有个醉醺醺的家伙拎着装满全新电脑设备的袋子。他谈到了他赚了多少钱。

00:45:52.000 --> 00:46:09.130
<v Ira Glass>A woman who just quit the AmeriCorps program hours before because it seemed too dangerous, and they wouldn't even give her a phone. There was a guy in Harlem who fixed up two of the patients in the optometrist's office where he works. A woman who was mad at the teacher who hit her son in school. A well-dressed man who explained the intricacies of estate tax assessment.
<v Ira Glass>还有一位女士，几小时前刚辞去了美国志愿队的工作，因为那看起来太危险了，他们甚至不给她配一部电话。还有哈勒姆的一个家伙，他在工作的眼科诊所修复了两位病人的视力。一位对在学校打她儿子的老师感到愤怒的女士。一位衣着考究的男士，他解释了遗产税评估的错综复杂。

00:46:09.130 --> 00:46:13.720
<v Ira Glass>And there was this guy, who walked up to Liz and Bill right after being let go from his job.
<v Ira Glass>还有一个刚被解雇后走向利兹和比尔的家伙。

00:46:13.720 --> 00:46:33.860
<v Man 2>They brought me into an office and they just said, "As you know, things have been very slow, and I really don't know how to tell you this. I mean, you've been great. It's not a firing. It's purely economic-based." But I feel like-- have you ever been laid off or fired?
<v Man 2>他们把我叫进办公室，只是说：“你知道，情况一直很缓慢，我真的不知道该怎么告诉你。我的意思是，你一直很棒。这不是解雇。这纯粹是出于经济原因。”但我感觉——你们曾经被解雇或辞退过吗？

00:46:33.860 --> 00:46:34.630
<v Bill>Yes.
<v Bill>是的。

00:46:34.630 --> 00:46:41.090
<v Man 2>You know that blank feeling you get right here? It's just like, oh, my god.
<v Man 2>你知道你这里会有的那种空白感吧？就像，噢，天哪。

00:46:41.090 --> 00:46:45.130
<v Bill>Do you have any kind of savings? Are you in a rent-stabilized place?
<v Bill>你有存款吗？你住在租金稳定的地方吗？

00:46:45.130 --> 00:46:45.970
<v Man 2>Yeah. No, I'm in a rent-stabilized place.
<v Man 2>是的。不，我住在租金稳定的地方。

00:46:45.970 --> 00:47:03.310
<v Ira Glass>As conversationalists, Bill and Liz are perfectly fine, no better or worse than you and me. Mostly what they do is keep the ball rolling. Watching all this for several hours, it makes you start to see everyone on the street differently. Everyone starts to seem like he or she could suddenly burst into a story. A whole city seems filled with people who need to get something off their chests.
<v Ira Glass>作为谈话者，比尔和利兹表现得非常好，不比你我更好或更差。他们主要做的是保持对话进行下去。观察这一切几个小时，让你开始以不同的眼光看待街上的每个人。每个人看起来都好像突然会吐露一个故事。整座城市似乎挤满了需要倾诉心声的人。

00:47:03.310 --> 00:47:11.170
<v Man 2>At this point, it's also, I want just some more stability in my life. And my birthday's next week. I'm going to be 39 years old.
<v Man 2>在这一点上，我还想要生活中的稳定感。我下周生日。我就要39岁了。

00:47:11.170 --> 00:47:45.220
<v Ira Glass>At the end of day, a man talks to them for three hours. Three hours. Mostly about a girlfriend that he lost who he can't get over, but also about the war and the time, years ago, that he tried to kill himself. By the end, it was 1:00 in the morning and he offered Liz and Bill $100, which they turned down. Liz was still full of energy and completely cheery. It seemed like she could go for another three hours. But it was late and the streets were clearing, and it was time to go home.
<v Ira Glass>一天结束时，一个男人和他们聊了三个小时。三个小时。主要关于他失去的那个无法释怀的女友，但也谈到了战争，谈到多年前他试图自杀的那次经历。到最后，已经是凌晨1点了，他给了利兹和比尔100美元，他们拒绝了。利兹依然精力充沛，完全兴致勃勃。看起来她可以再聊三个小时。但天色已晚，街道也空了，是时候回家了。

00:47:45.220 --> 00:48:03.590
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "TALK TO ME" BY HEATHER NOVA]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 海瑟·诺娃演唱的《跟我聊聊》]

00:48:03.590 --> 00:48:17.820
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, Wedding Bells and Telephone Bells. We end our program today with two brief case studies of situations where the words for something don't even matter, where it's not about the words at all. We begin with this one from Elizabeth Gilbert.
<v Ira Glass>第四章，婚礼的钟声与电话的铃声。我们以两个简短的案例研究结束今天的节目，探讨语言在某些情况下根本不重要，因为这根本与言语无关。我们从伊丽莎白·吉尔伯特带来的这一个开始。

00:48:17.820 --> 00:48:33.080
<v Elizabeth Gilbert>My friend Kevin once attended a wedding where he heard the world's most inappropriate wedding toast. The toast began inauspiciously enough. The best man stood up during the meal, clinked his knife against the crystal, and the other guests all quieted down.
<v Elizabeth Gilbert>我的朋友凯文曾参加过一个婚礼，在那里他听到了世界上最不合时宜的婚礼祝酒词。祝酒词的开头还算平稳。伴郎在用餐期间站起来，用刀轻敲水晶杯，其他宾客都安静了下来。

00:48:33.080 --> 00:48:51.980
<v Elizabeth Gilbert>"I was thinking on the airplane ride here about what I was going to say today about Danny and Joyce," the best man began. "And all I could think at first was what a happy day today is." Good enough start. But then this speech took an interesting turn. "And I realized that what I really wanted to talk about this afternoon is jury duty."
<v Elizabeth Gilbert>“我在飞往这里的飞机上一直在想今天关于丹尼和乔伊斯我要说些什么，”伴郎开始说。“起初我脑子里想到的只有今天是一个多么快乐的日子。”开场不错。但随后这段演讲发生了有趣的转折。“然后我意识到，今天下午我真正想聊的是陪审团义务。”

00:48:51.980 --> 00:49:20.210
<v Elizabeth Gilbert>"Now, I don't know how many of you have ever served on a jury," he went on. "But it's a fascinating process. I was just on a jury last year for the first time in my life, and I learned a lot about myself and about the legal system. It was a pretty serious case, too. It was actually a murder trial. It was very tragic. It was this old man who got killed. Very sad. He was getting money out of an ATM in the middle of the day and some gang kids came up and robbed him and shot him right in the face."
<v Elizabeth Gilbert>“现在，我不知道你们有多少人担任过陪审员，”他接着说。“但这是一个迷人的过程。去年我人生中第一次当陪审员，我对自己和法律制度学到了很多。那也是一个相当严重的案件。实际上是一起谋杀案审判。非常悲惨。是一个老人在大白天从ATM机取钱时被一群帮派孩子抢劫并直接朝脸上开了枪。”

00:49:20.210 --> 00:49:43.670
<v Elizabeth Gilbert>By now, many of the wedding guests were lowering their champagne glasses gently back down to the table. "It was a cut-and-dry case, really," he went on. "There were plenty of witnesses, and the forensic evidence pointed straight to one kid as the shooter. The kid was definitely guilty. But here's the thing. It was actually a capital offense. And my jury had to decide whether or not to give this kid the death penalty."
<v Elizabeth Gilbert>此时，许多婚礼宾客正轻轻地将香槟杯放回桌上。“那真是一个证据确凿的案件，”他继续说道。“有大量的证人，法医证据直接指向其中一个孩子是枪手。那孩子绝对是有罪的。但问题是。这实际上是死刑案件。我的陪审团不得不决定是否判处这个孩子死刑。”

00:49:43.670 --> 00:50:01.110
<v Elizabeth Gilbert>"Now, I don't know if any of you have ever had to decide whether somebody should live or die, but it's emotionally intense. We all knew the kid was guilty, but the death penalty is nothing to take lightly. In the end, though, we decided yes, this kid needs to die. And we sent him to his death."
<v Elizabeth Gilbert>“现在，我不知道你们是否有人曾经不得不决定某人该生还是该死，但这在情感上非常强烈。我们都知道那孩子有罪，但死刑不是可以轻率对待的事情。不过最终，我们决定是的，这个孩子必须死。我们将他送上了死路。”

00:50:01.110 --> 00:50:32.270
<v Elizabeth Gilbert>The tent was silent. The bride, ashen. The best man took a moment to compose himself and concluded, "That was probably the worst day of my life. And I got to thinking about it on the plane because that day was nothing like today, which is a happy day. A really happy day. So here's to Danny and Joyce." Thus concluded the toast.
<v Elizabeth Gilbert>帐篷里一片寂静。新娘脸色煞白。伴郎花了一点时间平复情绪，总结道：“那可能是我一生中最糟糕的一天。我在飞机上一直在想这件事，因为那一天和今天完全不同，今天是快乐的一天。真正快乐的一天。所以，敬丹尼和乔伊斯。”祝酒词至此结束。

00:50:32.270 --> 00:51:03.170
<v Elizabeth Gilbert>I've pondered the meaning of this story for years, and ultimately, I've decided that I get it. I've heard it said before that the human psyche cannot always tell the difference between good events and bad events. All we can feel is the tremor of the earth. Which is what happened to our best man, I believe. He was so overcome by happiness for his friend and he was so out of touch with his emotions that he couldn't express that happiness appropriately. All he could do was remember the last time he had felt so moved by something, and so he tried to express that.
<v Elizabeth Gilbert>我多年来一直在思考这个故事的含义，最终，我觉得我懂了。我以前听人说过，人类的心理并不总能区分好事和坏事。我们所能感受到的只有大地的震颤。我相信这就是发生在我们这位伴郎身上的事情。他被朋友的快乐深深打动，以至于他与自己的情绪脱节，无法适当地表达那种快乐。他所能做的只是回忆起上一次他因某事而如此感动时的情景，所以他试图表达出来。

00:51:03.170 --> 00:51:34.380
<v Elizabeth Gilbert>Sure, there's nothing parallel about an old man getting shot in the face and a dear friend getting married-- unless, of course, you measure human emotion by the weight, in which case, the two events carry exactly the same impact. Which is to say that I think I finally understand what the best man was trying to convey that afternoon. And I raise my glass to the poor guy for his valiant and hopeless attempt to celebrate.
<v Elizabeth Gilbert>当然，老人被枪击脸部和挚友结婚之间没有任何相似之处——除非，你以重量来衡量人类情感，在这种情况下，这两个事件带来的冲击力完全相同。也就是说，我想我终于明白了伴郎那天下午想要表达的内容。我举杯向这家伙致意，为了他那徒劳且毫无希望的庆祝尝试。

00:51:34.380 --> 00:51:43.830
<v Ira Glass>Liz Gilbert is the author of many books, including most recently, Big Magic. Her story first appeared on a website that doesn't exist anymore but was called otherpeoplesstories.com.
<v Ira Glass>利兹·吉尔伯特是许多书的作者，包括最近出版的《大魔法》。她的故事最初出现在一个已经不存在但名为otherpeoplesstories.com的网站上。

00:51:43.830 --> 00:51:50.590
<v Ira Glass>Well, now we move onto our next example of wordless communication. Our very last example today from Jonathan Goldstein.
<v Ira Glass>好的，现在我们转向下一个无言交流的例子。我们今天的最后一个例子来自乔纳森·戈尔茨坦。

00:51:50.590 --> 00:52:11.060
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Hettie lives in the same building she grew up in, and I live with her. We're in an apartment two floors up from her dad. He's 78 years old and doesn't want to bother climbing up the stairs every time he wants her, so he ends up usually just calling her on the phone. He calls anywhere from 20 to 30 times a day. The one thing he never wants is conversation.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>赫蒂住在他长大的那栋楼里，我和她住在一起。我们住在楼上两层，离她爸爸不远。他78岁了，不想每次想要找她时都费力爬楼梯，所以他通常最后只是给她打电话。他每天打20到30次电话。他唯一不想要的就是谈话。

00:52:11.060 --> 00:52:50.770
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Sometimes he calls to ask Hettie what time it is because he's too tired to get up and look for himself. Sometimes he calls to see what she's having for dinner. But most of the time, he calls and doesn't say a thing. Hettie picks up the phone and only hears classical music playing and she knows it's her dad with his kitchen radio on. Sometimes I'll walk into the living room and Hettie will be watching TV with the telephone cupped to her ear, not saying a word, and I know she's on the phone with her dad, and that, three floors down, he's sitting, watching TV just as silently.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>有时他打电话问赫蒂几点了，因为他太累了，起不来自己看。有时他打电话看她晚餐吃什么。但大部分时间，他打电话过来却什么也不说。赫蒂拿起电话只能听到古典音乐在播放，她知道那是她爸爸开了厨房里的收音机。有时我走进客厅，赫蒂正看着电视，电话捂在耳边，一言不发，我知道她正跟她爸爸通电话，而在三层楼下，他也同样安静地坐着看电视。

00:52:50.770 --> 00:53:17.110
<v Jonathan Goldstein>When our line is in use, the voice mail picks up. Hettie's father will leave a dozen messages in a row. The way we know it's him is that there are no words, just the click of the phone. After about 10 minutes, the fact that he can't get through starts to drive him crazy, so he walks down to the buzzers by the building's main door and buzzes our apartment. The way he sees it, why should he have to walk up two flights of stairs when he could just walk down one?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>当我们线路占用时，语音信箱会自动接入。赫蒂的父亲会接连留下十几条留言。我们知道是他，是因为没有话语，只有电话的点击声。大约10分钟后，打不进电话的事实开始让他抓狂，所以他会走到楼内正门处的门铃旁，按响我们的公寓。在他看来，既然可以只走一层楼梯，为什么非要走两层呢？

00:53:17.110 --> 00:53:46.640
<v Jonathan Goldstein>The buzzer is like an amplified dentist drill. And sometimes, when you're quiet or deep in thought, the suddenness of it is like being goosed by something cold and metallic. It is the kind of sound that rats in lab experiments come to associate with a terrible, perhaps fatal, error. When we hear the buzzer, we get off the internet or the telephone so that he could call. Even now it could buzz, you think to yourself. Or even now.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>那个门铃声就像被放大的牙医电钻。有时，当你安静或陷入深思时，那突如其来的声音就像被某种冰冷的金属物体戳了一下。这是实验室老鼠会将其与某种可怕的、甚至是致命的错误联系起来的那种声音。当我们听到门铃声，我们会退出互联网或电话，以便他可以打进来。即使现在它也可能响，你会这样想。或者即使是现在。

00:53:46.640 --> 00:54:15.710
<v Jonathan Goldstein>One day, I didn't get off the internet quick enough, so he went back down to the buzzer, and this time, he just leaned on it. I got off the line and Hettie phoned him. He wasn't answering. She yelled down the stairs, but still he would not stop. He was making a point. I am not exaggerating when I say that he kept the buzzer going for several minutes straight. And after a while, you could hear layered fluctuations and subtle pitch blends. He was like Yoko Ono on that thing.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>有一天，我没来得及迅速退出网络，所以他又回到门铃处，这一次，他只是靠在上面一直按着。我退出了线路，赫蒂给他打了过去。他不接。她对着楼梯大喊，但他还是不停手。他是在强调某点。我不夸张地说，他连续按了好几分钟门铃。过了一会儿，你可以听到层叠的波动和微妙的音调混合。他简直就像那个东西上的小野洋子。

00:54:15.710 --> 00:54:45.730
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Hettie got on a chair and hit the box above the door with a hammer. The buzzing stopped. A few minutes later, her father called. "What was so important?" Hettie asked. Her father was quiet. Finally, he asked her if she knew what night the Oscars were on. Hettie told him that he had gone too far.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>赫蒂站在椅子上用锤子砸向门上的盒子。嗡嗡声停止了。几分钟后，她父亲打来电话。“什么事这么重要？”赫蒂问。她父亲沉默了。最后，他问她知不知道奥斯卡颁奖典礼是哪晚。赫蒂告诉他，他太过分了。

00:54:45.730 --> 00:55:17.080
<v Jonathan Goldstein>It made Hettie's father sad that our doorbell was gone. He suggested that he and Hettie get walkie-talkies. I imagined them both sitting in their separate kitchens, each eating a sandwich, and in between long silences, one of them uttering the occasional 10-4. Hettie refused the offer. "Fine," he said. "From now on, I'll just go down into the basement and turn off the building's power two times really fast. I noticed where they keep the main switch the other day. That'll be the signal for you to free up the line."
<v Jonathan Goldstein>门铃坏了让赫蒂的父亲感到难过。他建议他和赫蒂买对讲机。我想象着他们各自坐在厨房里，一人吃着三明治，在长久的沉默中，偶尔有一方说一句“收到”。赫蒂拒绝了。 “好吧，”他说，“从现在起，我就下到地下室，把整栋楼的电源连续切断两次。我那天注意到他们把总开关放在哪里了。那将是给你的信号，让你腾出线路。”

00:55:17.080 --> 00:55:45.900
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Hettie told him that that would be the signal for her to move out. Now her father just comes up and knocks. He knocks in a particular way that I think is supposed to be a secret code. Often, after Hettie has answered the door, he just stands there in the doorway, looking at her uncertainly, sort of put upon that now that he's got her attention. He actually has to come up with something to say.
<v Jonathan Goldstein>赫蒂告诉他，那将是她搬出去的信号。现在她父亲只是走上来敲门。他敲门的方式很特别，我想那应该是一个秘密代码。通常，赫蒂开门后，他只是站在门口，不确定地看着她，好像一旦引起了她的注意，他反而不知所措。他实际上不得不找点什么话说。

00:55:45.900 --> 00:55:50.250
<v Ira Glass>Jonathan Goldstein is the host of the podcast Heavyweight from Gimlet Media.
<v Ira Glass>乔纳森·戈尔茨坦是Gimlet Media旗下播客《重量级》的主持人。

